WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.368 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-So I want to ask this.

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:02.569 size:81% position:41% line:92%
Recently, Sean Spicer,

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:04.571 align:start size:94% position:6%
the President's
first press secretary,

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:05.805 align:start size:94% position:6%
returned to
the press briefing room

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:09.242 align:start size:94% position:6%
to ask a question for
the news organization Newsmax.

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:11.044 align:start size:94% position:6%
Was that strange
for you and your colleague

00:00:11.044 --> 00:00:13.913 align:start size:94% position:6%
to see someone who had bee
behind the podium

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:16.549
you know, sort of sitting
in the pen with you guys?

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:18.885 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It was definitely surreal
because he, I think,

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:22.122
is the first White House
press secretary to then question

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:25.325
as a member of the media the
same President that he served.

00:00:25.325 --> 00:00:28.628 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it was very surprising.
I will tell you a quick story.

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:31.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because I literally
clean everything like crazy,

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:33.166 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was sitting
next to Sean Spicer,

00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:36.436 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I was cleaning my chai
so hard and so long

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:37.670 line:92%
that I literally did not realize

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.539 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Sean Spicer was sitting
next to me,

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.141 align:start size:94% position:6%
until he was like, "Hi,"
and I was like,

00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:42.742 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Oh, my gosh!
It's Sean Spicer."

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:45.345 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think that tells you
what moment we're living in,

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:47.380 size:81% position:41%
that we're so crazy,
I'm wiping down things

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
with hand sanitizer
and have to sit next to

00:00:50.116 --> 00:00:51.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
and am sitting next to
the former

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:54.354
White House press secretary,
who's now a member of the media.

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:57.957 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We have a newly former
White House press secretar

00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:00.326 align:start size:94% position:6%
joining the ranks --
that's Stephanie Grisham -

00:01:00.326 --> 00:01:02.662 line:92%
who, in her time having the job,

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:04.364 align:start size:94% position:6%
did not hold
a single press conference.

00:01:04.364 --> 00:01:08.401
What do you think that say
in regards to the strategy

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:09.769 align:start size:94% position:6%
this administration
seems to have

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:12.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
with their relationship
to the press?

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:13.973 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, Stephanie Grisham
never held

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:15.175 line:92%
a White House press briefing

00:01:15.175 --> 00:01:17.944
because President Trump,
frankly, didn't want her t

00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:20.180 size:78% position:39%
and didn't see a need
for press briefings.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:22.649
And even when he now
sees a need for press briefings,

00:01:22.649 --> 00:01:25.685
largely because this is th
number-one way that he's abl

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:28.188
to speak to the media
and speak to the American people

00:01:28.188 --> 00:01:32.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he now thinks
it's an important thing.

00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:33.793 line:92%
But I think what it tells us

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:36.096 size:78% position:39%
is that the President
understands media.

00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:37.297 line:92%
He understands prime time.

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:39.966 align:start size:94% position:6%
He understands how to choose
what he's saying.

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:42.202
So I think that it tells you
that the White House briefin

00:01:42.202 --> 00:01:44.104 align:start size:94% position:6%
just wasn't important
until he could no longer

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:46.339 align:start size:94% position:6%
hold rallies
and can no longer hold other

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:49.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
kind of alternate medias,
and this is the number-one way

00:01:49.342 --> 00:01:50.410 line:92%
that he's able to get coverage

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:54.814 align:start size:94% position:6%
-There was a lot of talk
even before the last election,

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:55.882 line:92%
"How do you cover a Trump rally?

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:59.419 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is it the right thing
to put it on television,

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:01.154 align:start size:94% position:6%
unedited, live,
as it's happening?"

00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:03.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
The same question now
is being asked

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:05.492 line:92%
about these press conferences.

00:02:05.492 --> 00:02:07.227 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know,
there are some media critics

00:02:07.227 --> 00:02:09.195 align:start size:94% position:6%
who don't think they should be
on TV every night.

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:11.231 align:start size:94% position:6%
As somebody who's in that room
you know, how --

00:02:11.231 --> 00:02:14.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
what do you think
is the right way to presen

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:17.203 size:78% position:39%
those press briefings
as they're happening?

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:18.972 line:92%
-So, it's really not my jo

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:21.074 align:start size:94% position:6%
to figure out whether or not
we cover them live.

00:02:21.074 --> 00:02:23.176 size:84% position:42%
What I can say is that,
when I'm covering them,

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:26.913
I try to add as much context
and fact-checking as possible,

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:29.382 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I think,
covering President Trump,

00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:31.084 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've learned
you have to have context.

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:32.852
You have to tell people,
"Here's what the facts are

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:35.288
and here's when he's wrong
and here's when he's right."

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:38.091 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think for people like mysel
who watch at home,

00:02:38.091 --> 00:02:42.662
one thing we enjoy to see --
enjoy seeing, excuse me --

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:44.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that sometimes the Presiden
will cut off a reporter

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:47.066 align:start size:94% position:6%
without asking --
answering a question.

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:49.802 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the next reporter will the
follow-up on behalf

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:52.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
of their colleague
who had the previous question.

00:02:52.872 --> 00:02:54.340 line:92%
Do you wish that happened more

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:58.645 align:start size:94% position:6%
and do you think it's importan
for the press to be unifie

00:02:58.645 --> 00:03:02.482 align:start size:94% position:6%
in those moments
where one will get cut off

00:03:02.482 --> 00:03:03.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think it's important
to follow-up

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:05.185 align:start size:94% position:6%
on each other's questions,
for sure.

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:07.620 align:start size:94% position:6%
Especially, because some o
the questions that we're askin

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.290 size:88% position:44%
are the basic questions.
The question is still,

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.725 size:75% position:38%
"If you need a test,
can you get a test?

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:14.460 line:92%
Will we have enough ventilators?

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.528 line:92%
What is the President doin

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:17.430 align:start size:94% position:6%
to make sure that people
are gonna stay safe?

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.532 align:start size:94% position:6%
When is the White House
gonna say, 'You know what?

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:20.900 align:start size:94% position:6%
Maybe the government
can open back up

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:23.203 size:81% position:41%
and we can all go back
to our normal lives?'

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:25.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
How many people
do we think are gonna die?

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:27.373 align:start size:94% position:6%
Those are basic questions
that, at times,

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:28.241 line:92%
we're not getting answers to

00:03:28.241 --> 00:03:32.412
So I think those questions can
and should continue to be asked.

00:03:32.412 --> 00:03:34.948 align:start size:94% position:6%
-This is, obviously,
a case where the President

00:03:34.948 --> 00:03:38.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
has been aggressive
with all walks of life

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:40.587 size:84% position:42% line:92%
if they're journalists.

00:03:40.587 --> 00:03:42.956 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yet it has been pointed ou
that he has been

00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:46.259 size:84% position:42%
particularly aggressive
with female journalists

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:47.327 line:92%
and with journalists of color.

00:03:47.327 --> 00:03:48.861 align:start size:94% position:6%
Obviously,
you tick both those boxes.

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:51.464 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you feel
that there's truth to that

00:03:51.464 --> 00:03:55.168
And what do you think that
again, says about this momen

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:55.868 size:78% position:39% line:92%
we're living through?

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:58.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think the President
wants to have an opponent,

00:03:58.004 --> 00:04:00.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he wants
to have a foil at all times.

00:04:00.473 --> 00:04:02.342 align:start size:94% position:6%
And whether or not that's
going after me

00:04:02.342 --> 00:04:04.077 align:start size:94% position:6%
or going after
Jonathan Karl of ABC

00:04:04.077 --> 00:04:06.546 align:start size:94% position:6%
or Peter Alexander of NBC,
he just, at times,

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:09.249 size:84% position:42%
I think, wants a fight,
wants a punching bag.

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:10.650 line:92%
And as we get into the fall,

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:11.818 align:start size:94% position:6%
he's gonna then turn
to Joe Biden.

00:04:11.818 --> 00:04:14.354 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's gonna turn to the media
that's been, of course,

00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:15.321 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
a long foil of his.

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:17.056 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I think in some ways,
I'm not sure

00:04:17.056 --> 00:04:19.993 size:81% position:41%
if he's particularly
picking on black women

00:04:19.993 --> 00:04:22.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
as much as he's
particularly picking on anyone

00:04:22.395 --> 00:04:23.730 line:92%
that he sees as a threat.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I really genuinely
want to thank you

00:04:26.266 --> 00:04:28.067 align:start size:94% position:6%
for bringing the integrity
that you bring

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:29.669 align:start size:94% position:6%
to those White House
press briefings.

00:04:29.669 --> 00:04:30.336 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
We're really lucky

00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:31.604 align:start size:94% position:6%
to have journalists like you
in the room.

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:34.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
And thank you so much
for making time for us tonight

00:04:34.207 --> 00:04:36.175 line:92%
-Thanks so much for having me.

